std.regex — Regular expression engine
======================================

A POSIX-conformant regular expression engine for Sysl,
implemented as a Pike VM (an extension of Thompson's NFA
construction that tracks capture group positions). The Pike
VM guarantees $O(n \cdot m)$ time complexity where $n$ is
the input length and $m$ is the pattern size — no
exponential backtracking.

The engine supports:

- **BRE** (Basic Regular Expressions) — the default mode,
  used by `grep`, `sed`, and `expr`
- **ERE** (Extended Regular Expressions) — planned, used by
  `egrep` and `awk`
- **POSIX leftmost-longest** match semantics
- **Capture groups** — `\(` and `\)` in BRE
- **Character classes** — `[abc]`, `[a-z]`, `[[:alpha:]]`
- **Anchors** — `^` and `$`
- **Repetition** — `*`, `\{n,m\}` in BRE

Architecture:

```
pattern string ──→ [Parser] ──→ AST ──→ [Compiler] ──→ VM bytecode ──→ [VM] ──→ match result
                                 │                        │
                           flat array of              flat array of
                           Node structs               Inst structs
```

The AST and VM instructions are stored in flat arrays with
integer indices for child references, avoiding the need for
recursive types or pointer-based trees.


Module declaration and imports
------------------------------

    module std.regex


Public types
------------

A compiled regular expression is an opaque handle containing
the VM bytecode and metadata.

    struct Regex
        insts: &[]Inst
        n_groups: int

A successful match returns capture group positions. Each
group has a start and end index into the input string.
Group 0 is always the full match.

    struct Match
        starts: &[]int
        ends: &[]int
        n_groups: int

    Match.group(self: *Match, i: int) -> (int, int)
        (self.starts[i], self.ends[i])

    Match.matched(self: *Match) -> bool = self.starts[0] >= 0


AST node representation
-----------------------

Since Sysl does not yet support recursive types, the AST is
a flat array of `Node` structs. Each node references its
children by index into the array. An index of `-1` means
"no child."

The `kind` field selects the node type. We use integer
constants rather than an enum so the struct stays simple and
flat.

    /* Node kind constants */
    val NK_LITERAL = 0      /* single character */
    val NK_DOT = 1          /* . (any character) */
    val NK_ANCHOR_START = 2 /* ^ */
    val NK_ANCHOR_END = 3   /* $ */
    val NK_CLASS = 4        /* [...] character class */
    val NK_STAR = 5         /* * (zero or more of left) */
    val NK_CAPTURE = 6      /* \(...\) group; extra = group number */
    val NK_SEQ = 7          /* concatenation: left then right */
    val NK_QUEST = 8        /* \? (zero or one of left) — BRE \{0,1\} */
    val NK_PLUS = 9         /* \+ (one or more of left) — BRE \{1,\} */

    struct Node
        kind: int
        ch: int             /* character for NK_LITERAL */
        left: int           /* child index, or -1 */
        right: int          /* child index (for NK_SEQ), or -1 */
        extra: int          /* group number (NK_CAPTURE), class id (NK_CLASS) */

Character classes are stored separately — a class is a list
of ranges. Each range is a `(low, high)` pair inclusive.
The class struct also tracks negation (`[^...]`).

    struct CharRange
        lo: int
        hi: int

    struct CharClass
        ranges: &[]CharRange
        negated: bool


VM instruction set
------------------

The Pike VM executes a linear array of instructions. Each
instruction is one struct with an opcode and up to two
operands. The instruction set is minimal — just enough to
implement all regex constructs.

    /* Instruction opcodes */
    val OP_CHAR = 0         /* match literal character (arg1 = char) */
    val OP_ANY = 1          /* match any character (.) */
    val OP_MATCH = 2        /* successful match — stop */
    val OP_JUMP = 3         /* unconditional jump (arg1 = target pc) */
    val OP_SPLIT = 4        /* fork: try arg1, queue arg2 */
    val OP_SAVE = 5         /* save position into capture slot arg1 */
    val OP_ANCHOR_START = 6 /* assert at start of string */
    val OP_ANCHOR_END = 7   /* assert at end of string */
    val OP_CLASS = 8        /* match character class (arg1 = class index) */

    struct Inst
        op: int
        arg1: int
        arg2: int

The `SPLIT` instruction is the heart of the NFA simulation.
It represents a branch point: the VM adds *both* targets to
its work list, preferring `arg1` (the "greedy" path). For
POSIX leftmost-longest semantics, the VM tries all paths
and keeps the longest match.


Error type
----------

Parse errors carry the position in the pattern and a
description.

    struct RegexError
        pos: int
        msg: string


Public API
----------

These are the entry points. The engine is used in two steps:
compile a pattern, then execute it against input strings.

Compilation parses the pattern and produces VM bytecode.
It can fail if the pattern has syntax errors.

    /* Compile a BRE pattern. Returns a compiled Regex or an error. */
    /* compile_bre(pattern: string) -> Result[Regex, RegexError] */

    /* Execute a compiled regex against an input string. */
    /* Returns a Match on success, or nothing. */
    /* exec(re: *Regex, input: string) -> Result[Match, int] */

    /* Convenience: compile and execute in one step. */
    /* match_bre(pattern: string, input: string) -> Result[Match, RegexError] */

> **Note:** The function bodies are in the `parser`, `compiler`,
> and `vm` submodules. They are left as signatures here until
> cross-module re-export is wired up.
